WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:11.264
The payments program keeps its notes and counters in Redis, a program that holds data in memory and answers very fast.

2
00:00:11.264 --> 00:00:18.521
It has grown to 40 GB and 300,000 commands a second, and one Redis runs every command one at a time.

3
00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.973
So the keys get split over three servers, and each one owns a part.

4
00:00:22.973 --> 00:00:25.141
Now your program wants payment:42.

5
00:00:25.141 --> 00:00:26.672
Which server do you ask?

6
00:00:26.672 --> 00:00:29.351
And what happens if you ask the wrong one?

7
00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.222
Picture a cloakroom with 16,384 numbered hooks and three attendants.

8
00:00:34.222 --> 00:00:36.931
Your ticket always picks the same hook.

9
00:00:36.931 --> 00:00:41.723
And a board by the door says which attendant looks after which hooks.

10
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:44.910
Redis Cluster works the same way.

11
00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:47.828
The hooks are called hash slots.

12
00:00:47.828 --> 00:00:56.128
A key's slot is a checksum of the key, mod 16,384, so payment:42 always lands in slot 9055.

13
00:00:56.300 --> 00:01:02.676
The attendants are the servers, called nodes, each with its own address.

14
00:01:02.676 --> 00:01:05.510
And the board is the slot table.

15
00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:12.064
Node two looks after slots 5461 to 10922, so payment:42 lives on node two.

16
00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:19.021
Now take the simplest client, a program that knows one address and treats it as one Redis.

17
00:01:19.021 --> 00:01:20.537
Ours knows node one.

18
00:01:20.537 --> 00:01:25.993
It sends GET payment:42 to node one, and node one doesn't own slot 9055.

19
00:01:25.993 --> 00:01:27.661
What does node one do?

20
00:01:27.661 --> 00:01:28.873
Pause and guess.

21
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:36.919
A kind attendant would walk to the next counter and fetch your coat.

22
00:01:36.919 --> 00:01:38.644
A Redis node never does.

23
00:01:38.644 --> 00:01:43.889
Node one answers with an error: MOVED, slot 9055, and node two's address.

24
00:01:43.889 --> 00:01:46.332
It lives over there, so ask there.

25
00:01:46.467 --> 00:01:50.334
The client asks again, at node two, and gets its answer.

26
00:01:50.334 --> 00:01:50.679
Rude?

27
00:01:50.679 --> 00:01:51.301
A little.

28
00:01:51.301 --> 00:01:54.478
But that rudeness is why the cluster can grow.

29
00:01:54.478 --> 00:01:59.727
Each node only ever works on its own slots, never on errands for the others.

30
00:01:59.900 --> 00:02:06.499
Following every MOVED works, but each one costs an extra round trip: a message there, and an answer back.

31
00:02:06.499 --> 00:02:11.778
So if the plain client sends all its commands to node one, how many come back MOVED?

32
00:02:11.778 --> 00:02:12.784
Pause and guess.

33
00:02:15.967 --> 00:02:17.125
About two in three.

34
00:02:17.125 --> 00:02:25.054
Node one owns a third of the slots, so two keys in three belong to someone else, and each of those makes two trips instead of one.

35
00:02:25.054 --> 00:02:29.384
Wouldn't it be nice if the client could read the board before it asked?

36
00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:32.585
That's exactly what a cluster-aware client does.

37
00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:37.994
When it starts, it asks any node for the whole slot table, its own photo of the board.

38
00:02:37.994 --> 00:02:40.509
And it opens a connection to every node.

39
00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:44.313
Then it works out each key's slot itself.

40
00:02:44.313 --> 00:02:48.367
payment:42 is slot 9055: straight to node two.

41
00:02:48.367 --> 00:02:52.685
payment:43 is slot 13182: straight to node three.

42
00:02:52.685 --> 00:02:55.681
One round trip each, and no MOVED.

43
00:02:55.900 --> 00:02:57.037
So far so good.

44
00:02:57.037 --> 00:02:59.386
But a photo can go out of date.

45
00:02:59.386 --> 00:03:04.085
Say someone running the cluster moves slot 9055 to node three.

46
00:03:04.085 --> 00:03:07.041
The client's table still says node two.

47
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:13.420
So its next GET goes to node two, which answers MOVED, slot 9055, with node three's address.

48
00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:19.910
The client fixes its table, usually by loading all of it again, because slots rarely move alone.

49
00:03:19.910 --> 00:03:24.372
Then it asks node three, and from then on, it goes straight there.

50
00:03:24.567 --> 00:03:30.297
With a cluster-aware client, a MOVED is rare, and it means one thing: the table changed.

51
00:03:30.297 --> 00:03:34.009
A slot moved, or a replica took over from a dead primary.

52
00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:34.784
One trap.

53
00:03:34.784 --> 00:03:41.206
The client does the routing, so the address inside a MOVED must work from wherever the client runs.

54
00:03:41.206 --> 00:03:43.607
Each node announces just one address.

55
00:03:43.833 --> 00:03:55.780
Run the cluster in Docker, a tool that runs each server as a small separate machine on your laptop, and the nodes may announce addresses that only exist inside Docker.

56
00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.858
That's a board that sends guests down the staff corridor.

57
00:03:59.858 --> 00:04:05.080
The lab makes every node announce 127.0.0.1, which works from both sides.

58
00:04:05.267 --> 00:04:08.018
And one rule to carry into the next section.

59
00:04:08.018 --> 00:04:12.269
A cluster client splits a batch of commands into one batch per node.

60
00:04:12.269 --> 00:04:17.896
But a transaction, a group of commands that runs as one step, can't be split across nodes.

61
00:04:17.896 --> 00:04:19.459
Keep that in your pocket.

62
00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:25.063
In Java and Kotlin, you give the cluster client one address, the seed.

63
00:04:25.063 --> 00:04:29.837
It loads the table from there and sends each command to its owner.

64
00:04:29.837 --> 00:04:34.249
Both print the same line: payment:42, slot 9055, on node two.

65
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:36.839
So, which server do you ask?

66
00:04:36.839 --> 00:04:38.416
The one the table names.

67
00:04:38.416 --> 00:04:44.328
A key hashes to a slot, the table names the slot's node, and a node never fetches for you.

68
00:04:44.328 --> 00:04:45.971
It tells you where to go.

69
00:04:46.167 --> 00:04:51.315
Ask the wrong one, and it costs you one extra trip, and a fresh copy of the board.

70
00:04:51.767 --> 00:04:53.001
One quick check.

71
00:04:53.001 --> 00:04:56.705
A plain client sends GET payment:43 to node one.

72
00:04:56.705 --> 00:04:57.940
What comes back?

73
00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.175
Pause and think.

74
00:05:02.367 --> 00:05:06.739
MOVED, slot 13182, with node three's address.

75
00:05:06.739 --> 00:05:13.345
Slot 13182 is in node three's range, so the client asks again there.
